WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.194
A practical request-control baseline for ASP.

2
00:00:03.194 --> 00:00:05.252
NET Core begins with purpose.

3
00:00:05.252 --> 00:00:10.576
Rate limiting protects fairness, availability, cost, and backend resources.

4
00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:20.726
It should be based on endpoint behavior, user experience, tenant fairness, backend capacity, and operational visibility, not arbitrary numbers.

5
00:00:20.726 --> 00:00:24.985
Choose limiter algorithms based on the work being protected.

6
00:00:24.985 --> 00:00:32.296
Fixed windows, sliding windows, token buckets, and concurrency limiters each shape traffic differently.

7
00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:40.742
Expensive endpoints, bursty clients, and long-running operations may need different controls than cheap read endpoints.

8
00:00:40.742 --> 00:00:43.865
Partition fairly and scope policies clearly.

9
00:00:43.865 --> 00:00:52.241
Use stable, meaningful, privacy-aware keys such as user, tenant, API key, route, or client category where appropriate.

10
00:00:52.241 --> 00:00:59.836
Make global and endpoint-specific policies reviewable, and keep overrides rare, documented, and time-bound.

11
00:00:59.836 --> 00:01:03.172
Request controls include more than rate limits.

12
00:01:03.172 --> 00:01:17.225
Kestrel request body limits, connection limits, header timeouts, data-rate controls, upload workflows, decompression constraints, and hosting-layer limits all shape how much work the system accepts.

13
00:01:17.225 --> 00:01:22.904
Align those limits across the app, proxy, gateway, CDN, and hosting environment.

14
00:01:22.904 --> 00:01:25.033
Finally, operate the controls.

15
00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:33.764
Monitor rejections, queues, latency, timeouts, body rejections, tenant hotspots, endpoint hotspots, and backend saturation.

16
00:01:33.764 --> 00:01:37.455
Tune limits with safe testing and production review.

17
00:01:37.455 --> 00:01:44.694
Treat request controls as ongoing operational controls, not constants that are set once and forgotten.
